================================================================================
Non-commercial third-party (reference GPU pipeline)
================================================================================

The backend (backend/) wraps LAM and FLAME to generate `.splattie` files from
photos. These third-party components are restricted:

LAM (Large Avatar Model)
  Source: https://github.com/aigc3d/LAM
  License: research-only. See upstream LICENSE.
  Bundled as: git submodule at backend/vendor/LAM

FLAME (Faces Learned with an Articulated Model and Expressions)
  Source: https://flame.is.tue.mpg.de
  License: non-commercial research license. Requires attribution.
  Bundled as: model files downloaded by backend/scripts/setup-gpu.sh
  Commercial license must be negotiated with MPI for Intelligent Systems.

diff-gaussian-rasterization (INRIA)
  Source: https://github.com/graphdeco-inria/diff-gaussian-rasterization
  License: non-commercial. See upstream LICENSE.
  Used as: CUDA dependency of LAM.

================================================================================
Path to commercial use
================================================================================

You have three options for using Splattie commercially:

1. Use only the widget + format. Generate `.splattie` files through your own
   pipeline. The widget loads any `.splattie` that follows the spec - it does
   not require LAM or FLAME at runtime.

2. License LAM and FLAME commercially with their respective authors (and
   replace diff-gaussian-rasterization with gsplat, Apache 2.0).

3. Implement an alternative head-generation method behind the
   `HeadGenerationMethod` protocol (see backend/src/splattie/methods/).
   Suggested commercial-friendly stack:
     - gsplat (Apache 2.0) instead of diff-gaussian-rasterization
     - Your own head reconstruction model, or a commercially-licensed one
     - Your own face model, or a commercially-licensed one

The `.splattie` format itself is method-agnostic. `manifest.json.generator.method`
declares which method produced the file; the widget does not care.
